#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124308 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "922b0f79-2c87-56f9-bf8a-18711a2a09d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4838" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](images/bd/bd2cf58c26a51ae147b3714556631e1df786ae6e56cde01f801d9d93f7c258f5.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124309 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "a3911ca2-a357-5f37-8abc-e01e5518f24b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4840" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](images/15/15e0d0c04e0352356acab8001936031505931dac7eb7afefa1881dd333448e57.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](images/17/178769437e55f1822723ac4cc090efca62aac6be9e3ad9dc8f7971b8a6196484.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "a2d3629f-5b7c-5b65-998f-8754ff11e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4841" :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b395052cc098a6daeb50037d07223b20fb93163c10c31a432c7fb9c9a7804db.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](images/82/82e477164a2708a6f8e7f1142dce3e290d3aa216b5828e0523c16555533f02b7.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f04f55d3-40ec-52a8-a762-d8ac7090b8b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7276" :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c61cc387c58af544cf95641a1c2a28e5412d223fb03a7afe5157ee4eba3902bb.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/76/76d9c82037008323681ec9a0745b39740bf1405c767fc9aaee411cdd1fadde1a.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](images/48/481e6321dd23a8af4c59201685295862cc927731eeec31c122ec62bdf156d179.svg)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426822c2-147e-581d-8735-96a3bffe8321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c39bc95-ae02-5c17-9a57-15db094b94c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436238} {:answer/id #uuid "89f6cf40-819f-5c6b-aeaf-667b443a0b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436240} {:answer/id #uuid "e55ca324-fff5-5f74-ad7a-3c6af6f7f1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436244} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b888d-cda8-5e1c-bef9-2a98c4f74288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c39bc95-ae02-5c17-9a57-15db094b94c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436238} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1014636-1510-50f6-b488-e3fe8d24c13d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e4c9378-b054-5da6-b199-f46a138a46d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113603" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2a1713-45a0-5ac9-812e-efe047dbc9eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81b9dfb-ab90-58a1-beaa-86bd297087c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122547} {:answer/id #uuid "ddda41d9-4800-5801-a72e-34cc8a59cb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122548} {:answer/id #uuid "e13110b2-7e67-5dde-936c-92458290784b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122549} {:answer/id #uuid "5e56b9d1-daba-59a6-aa86-d69f7c293852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122550} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce2d694-d316-548e-827a-f7d0f6ae0de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122551}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2266d9-ab0c-52c9-ae63-a9f7e1dedcc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122546}] :question/id #uuid "5c2ddf2c-2a05-5bc8-8534-d8dcfb7338b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36484" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}$?\n\n$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in Another Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a308b62-90e7-523e-9e35-8a394977cd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122553} {:answer/id #uuid "a58c7ba5-7030-55cb-9e8a-e74b7c247633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122554} {:answer/id #uuid "f7be5821-2fdc-5889-a78d-2e11e42491a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122555} {:answer/id #uuid "26bff3f9-022f-530d-84a9-4d7dc2fbfd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122556} {:answer/id #uuid "270f7c2b-f4c6-5167-a776-64998adb3257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122557}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ad3cb3a8-d1d3-5bb4-a5ae-5420e039345f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122552}] :question/id #uuid "ebf7f82a-f5ff-583f-9de1-a9dafa71d6c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-36487" :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}, C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}, [x]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of $R^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee52fd3-6c79-5016-ade5-46e7bfbb557a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given the Inverse Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c59446c-928f-5c4a-a62e-55383c3a3ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122805} {:answer/id #uuid "a21ed5ec-9dcc-5453-8bec-584bcfacb2fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122806} {:answer/id #uuid "ad06bd27-d360-57d5-8aac-8cbdd901efc5" :answer/type {:db/id #